+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Codex is a terminal-based coding agent. This package includes the OpenBSD
platform patches, including V8/Code Mode, and installs the upstream names
and default ~/.codex state.

No local command sandbox is available. Managed sandbox profiles are
rejected, including profile changes during sessions. To authorize ordinary
user access explicitly, use:

    codex --sandbox danger-full-access

Commands, hooks and MCP servers then have the user's ordinary authority.
Approvals and the V8 isolate do not provide OS isolation for subprocesses.
Use a dedicated virtual machine when needed. external-sandbox declares an
external boundary; Codex does not create or verify it.

The app-server/daemon has no `--sandbox` switch of its own. For sessions
started through it, select the same explicit mode in ~/.codex/config.toml,
for example `sandbox_mode = "danger-full-access"`, or use a declared external
sandbox. Without an explicit non-managed profile, a session that requests the
default managed profile is rejected before hooks, MCP or shell setup run.

The daemon uses the installed executable, following the official port:

    codex app-server daemon start
    codex app-server daemon version
    codex app-server daemon restart
    codex app-server daemon stop

Automatic startup defaults to disabled, as in the official port. Its
upstream flag remains available. The daemon runs as the invoking user,
inherits the starting environment and does not use an rc.d service.
State lives under CODEX_HOME; sockets use /tmp/codex-daemon-<uid>.

Update with pkg_add -u and restart the daemon. Internal updates (`codex
update`), `--from-cli` replacement and the updater loop are rejected. No
separate daemon package or private manifest is installed in
CODEX_HOME/packages.

The installed binaries require the WXNEEDED property for V8 152.2.0. The
filesystem containing ${LOCALBASE} must allow wxallowed. Python and Bash are
runtime dependencies; Git is declared because repository and worktree features
execute it.
Optional npm plugins, MCP servers and hooks may require other separately
packaged executables. Plugin metadata, explicit installation, marketplace
operations and the upstream synchronisation paths are retained. The package
adds no hidden downloader or binary installer. Review plugin and MCP sources
before enabling executable content, and do not use automatic binary
installers as MCP launchers.

You may use OpenAI commercial services or misc/llama.cpp, for the last
one you should add into ~/.codex/config.toml:

	model_provider = "local"
	model = "local"
	web_search = "disabled"

	[model_providers.local]
	name = "llama-server"
	base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1"
	wire_api = "responses"

${PKGSTEM} uses a lot of memory, it starts with 256Mb per agent/worker
thread, and you may need to bump your limits (`ulimit -d`) or raise
datasize limit in used login class..

Also, ${PKGSTEM} may use a lot of open files, you may need to raise both
the system-wide maxfiles and used login class limits.

Secret Service storage needs a D-Bus session and compatible provider.
Protect CODEX_HOME and credential backups, including file-backed auth.
Browser login uses xdg-open when available; the URL is printed otherwise and
`codex login --device-auth` remains available. Shell snapshots are limited to
bash/zsh; the default OpenBSD shells run commands without a snapshot. Clipboard
access uses the X11 backend when DISPLAY is set. These optional helpers are not
added as runtime dependencies by this port.

Only static review has been performed. Compilation, generated package
contents, V8, daemon, PTY, keyring and permission rejection require actual
OpenBSD validation. No claim of runtime correctness is made here.
